<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huayna Capac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1527</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huayna Capac was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> at its greatest extent, from about 1493. He campaigned in the north, crushing the Caranqui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayambi">Cayambi</a> and making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> a northern capital. He died around 1527, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> brought by Europeans, and the war between his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> weakened the empire before the Spanish arrived.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huayna-capac.jpg" alt="Huayna Capac" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hp_inka11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Huayna Capac was the eleventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> of Tawantinsuyu, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a>, who ruled from 1471 to 1493 and had extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule north into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. The exact place and date of his birth are unknown; he was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, but may have been born in 1468 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomebamba">Tumebamba</a>, modern Cuenca, and spent part of his childhood there.</p><p>Huayna Capac's first wife was his full sister Coya Cusirimay, who bore no male heirs. He sired more than 50 legitimate sons and about 200 illegitimate children with other women, and took another sister, Araua Ocllo, as royal wife; their son Thupaq Kusi Wallpa became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>. His other sons included Ninan Cuyuchi, the crown prince, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The empire reached its fullest extent under Huayna Capac, stretching over much of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, with subjects of more than two hundred ethnic groups. He pushed the southern frontier into what is now Chile and Argentina and tried to annex lands in the north. According to the Ecuadorian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-velasco">Juan de Velasco</a> he absorbed the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> by marrying its queen, Paccha Duchicela. Fond of Ecuador, he spent most of his time there, founded cities such as Atuntaqui and rebuilt Quito as a second capital beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>.</p><p>He developed Cochabamba, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, as an agricultural and administrative centre with more than two thousand silos for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, greatly expanded the road network and had storehouses built along it so that food could be sent quickly to people in danger of starvation. He knew of the Spanish arrival off the coast of his empire as early as 1515.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Huayna Capac died in 1527. He had contracted a fever while campaigning in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, though some historians dispute this, and the fever may have been a European disease such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. Other historians doubt that smallpox was the cause, since the earliest accounts are vague or disagree and none of the descriptions of his mummy mentions pockmarks. His eldest son Ninan Cuyochi also died, and the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> over the succession followed. Atahualpa won it.</p><p>Huayna Capac's mummy was kept in his palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, and was discovered by the Spanish in 1559. Taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> with the mummies of ten other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rulers, it was displayed in the San Andrés Hospital and eventually buried or destroyed. A search beneath the hospital in 2001 found only an empty crypt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huayna Capac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Túpac Inca Yupanqui, first as general and from about 1471 as ruler, conquered the Kingdom of Chimor on the north coast and pushed the frontiers into Ecuador and south into Chile, while <b>Huayna Capac</b>, from about 1493, extended rule to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire, Francisco Pizarro executed Atahualpa in 1533, entered Cusco and installed Manco Inca, a son of <b>Huayna Capac</b>, as a puppet ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As many as 4,000 people were killed on the death of <b>Huayna Capac</b> in 1527.</blockquote><a href="/religion/inca-religion">Inca religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish chroniclers recorded that, in Inca oral history, the fortress was built during the reign of the Sapa Inca Pachacuti and his successors, Topa Inca Yupanqui and <b>Huayna Capac</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Andes an epidemic, probably smallpox, killed the Inca ruler <b>Huayna Capac</b> and his chosen heir in the late 1520s and opened a civil war between his sons Huáscar and Atahualpa, which was ending just as Pizarro arrived.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Huayna Capac</b> died around 1527, his sons Huáscar and Atahualpa fought a civil war (1529–1532), and in November 1532 the Spaniards captured the victor, Atahualpa, at Cajamarca.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomebamba">Tumebamba</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-2.91,-79&amp;year=1517" aria-label="Tumebamba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2357 2272 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Huayna Capac"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2807 2542h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2807 2542h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-2.91,-79&amp;year=1517">What was here in 1517? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
